    "q-161779": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161779",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=1$ in the first quadrant.",
      "worked_solution": "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4x\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}4x\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}4x[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=1}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4x(1-{x}^{2})\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14227375/assets/40624d9441af334e6025fccd70040cd7cf9eca972da4aa69a9f9a0133465a7be.png)\n\n \n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4x(1-{x}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}4x(1-{x}^{2})\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x(1-{x}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}1\\,dz]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x(1-{x}^{2})[z]_{z=0}^{z=1-{x}^{2}}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x(1-{x}^{2})(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x(1-{x}^{2})^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2x)(1-{x}^{2})^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{(1-{x}^{2})^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
